ence":92,"evidenceIds":["E12","E2"]},{"itemId":"I23","answer":"sufficient","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E8","E14","E15","E10"]}],"evidenceRequests":["Q3","Q2","Q1"],"summary":"The OTP decline is real and not a measurement artifact (E12 manual checks, E2). Groups A and B stepped down at the recovery cut; construction hit the five corridor routes in a second step (E8, E9). Group C, with neither the recovery cut nor construction, was flat. Group B's late departures come from buses that arrived with no layover (E10). The 40% absence rise is mostly an artifact of ShiftLine recording partial-day absences (E13, E14). Full-day absences rose 1.1%, and the dispatch log shows unchanged unfilled trips and late pull-outs (E15). Restore recovery time, set construction running times, and restate the absence trend. Do not adopt the attendance discipline program, and do not suspend OTP reporting. Early leave may partly reflect the layover cuts, but the evidence on that is confounded."}}